   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine rend="printed">
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI_09">71, rue du Cardinal Lemoine, V<hi rend="superscript">e</hi>
                                </name>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI">Paris</name> V</addrLine> </address>
                    </ab>
                </div>
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>Dear <name type="person" key="weav76">Miss Weaver</name>: I have received your letter which relieved me very much as I had been imagining all kinds of things about you to account for your preceding letter. If you allow me I shall write to you a fairly long <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">letter</seg> in reply to yours which will be perhaps a speech from the dock. May I also say that I am not at all offended by your letter, as you seem to suppose?</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>        	With kindest regards and friendly wishes</salute>
                        <salute>                    	sincerely yours</salute>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">                                	James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
                        <dateline>
                            <date when="1921-06-19">19.vi.921</date>
                        </dateline> </closer> </div> </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">Harriet Weaver had expressed alarm after hearing rumours about JJ’s excessive drinking habits (<name type="work">JJ</name> 509). In his extended reply (the “speech from the dock”; see <rs type="letter" key="L_68777">JJ to Harriet Weaver, 24 June 1921</rs> [<name type="work">SL</name> 281-84]), he enumerated preposterous “legends” that supposedly had been circulating about him in various countries and bemoaned the lack of support in Paris for his work.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
   </text>
